  "course": {
    "programmeTitle": "Bachelor of Engineering in Data Science and Engineering",
    "code": "LLM402",
    "title": "Large Language Model (LLM)",
    "credits": {
      "total": 3.0
    },
    "prerequisites": null,
    "courseType": "Core",
    "availability": {
      "semester": 2,
      "year": 4
    },
    "description": "This course provides a comprehensive introduction to Large Language Models (LLMs), bridging foundational concepts in language representation with modern advances in generative AI. Students begin by exploring the core principles behind LLM architectures, tokenization strategies, and pretraining objectives, before advancing to hands-on implementation of key techniques such as prompt engineering, fine-tuning, and model alignment. The curriculum covers essential applications of LLMs, including text generation, conversational agents, code generation, reasoning tasks, and multimodal integration, with a strong focus on leveraging state-of-the-art frameworks like PyTorch, Hugging Face Transformers, and advanced inference tools. Students will gain practical experience in building, adapting, and deploying LLM-based systems for real-world tasks, while also addressing challenges such as hallucination mitigation, scalability, computational efficiency, and domain adaptation. Finally, the course emphasizes ethical and responsible AI practices covering bias, fairness, security, and interpretability, and equips students with the skills to evaluate, optimize, and reliably deploy LLM systems across diverse applications."
  },
